"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.7. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pT2 stage, positive surgical margins, and absence of seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. The absence of removed lymph nodes, noted as pNx, widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy results showed a difference between the initial Gleason 0+0 and the later Gleason 3+4 with perineural invasion.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of extraprostatic extension and no seminal vesicle invasion. The positive surgical margins represent a risk factor. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly limits the ability to assess the subsequent trajectory of recurrence. The lack of nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of regional disease control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 80.98 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 52.64–109.33 months; its coverage has not been validated."
